\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Ordinary Least Squares Estimator (OLSE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract

\series bold
Estimation of Standard Linear Model
\series default
 - 
\series bold
Ordinary Least Squares Estimator
\series default
: Model specfication, objective function, finite sample and asymptotic propertie
s of the OLSE, measurement of Goodness of Fit, Tests of Linear Hypotheses,
 Restriced LSE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Standard Linear Regression Model (SLM)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Linear Regression Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The data in economics usually cannot be generated by experiments, so both
 the dependent and independent variables have to be treated as random variables,
 variables whose values are subject to chance.
 A 
\series bold
model
\series default
 is a set of restictions on the joint distribtuion of the dependent and
 independent variables.
 That is, a model is a set of joint distributions satisfying a set of assumption
s.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A linear regression model specifies the dependent variable (regressand)
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 as a sum of linear function of the 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 observable explanatory variables (regressors) 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{tk}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,2,\ldots,K$
\end_inset

, and an unobersable error term 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{t}=X_{t}\beta+u_{t},\ t=1,2,\ldots,N\label{eq:1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 indiciates the 
\begin_inset Formula $t^{th}$
\end_inset

 observation, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-dimensional row vector and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-dimensional column vector of unknonw coefficients.
 This is written in a matrix form for the entire 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 obersvations as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=X\beta+u
\]

\end_inset

where the dimensions of the matrices are 
\begin_inset Formula $y:N\times1,\ \beta:K\times1,\ \mbox{and}\ u:N\times1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) Estimation Method
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The OLS estimation method does not require information about the statistical
 properties of the variables in the model.
 It just needs to specify a linear (or nonlinear) regression equation.
 Properties of the OLS estimator of course depends on the statistical properties
 of the variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Although we do not observe the error term, we can calculate the value implied
 by a hypothetical value, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta},$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}-X_{t}\tilde{\beta}
\]

\end_inset

this is called the residual for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 From this, form the 
\series bold
sum of squared residuals
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $(SSR)$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
SSR\left(\tilde{\beta}\right)\equiv{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}(y_{t}-X_{t}\tilde{\beta})^{2}=(y-X\tilde{\beta})^{\prime}(y-X\tilde{\beta})\label{eq:2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is also called the 
\series bold
error sum of squares
\series default
 
\series bold
(ESS)
\series default
 or the 
\series bold
residual sum of squares
\series default
 
\series bold
(RSS)
\series default
.
 It is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 because the residual depends on it.
 The 
\series bold
OLS estimate
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

, of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 that minimizes this function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}\equiv\arg{\displaystyle \min_{\tilde{\beta}}}\ SSR\left(\tilde{\beta}\right)\label{eq:3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As an example, if 
\begin_inset Formula $K=2$
\end_inset

 and the first column of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is all 
\begin_inset Formula $1's$
\end_inset

, then we want to find 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{2}$
\end_inset

 such that the estimated regression line, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{t}=\hat{\beta}_{1}+x_{t}\hat{\beta}_{2}$
\end_inset

, is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

close
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to the data points.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Closeness
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is measured by the 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR$
\end_inset

.
 So, the least squares method of estimation of unknown coefficeints 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{k}$
\end_inset

 finds the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 that minimizes the sum of squared residuals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since it depends on the sample 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,X)$
\end_inset

, the OLS estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is in general different from the true value 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 By having squared residuals in the objective function, this method imposes
 a heavy penalty on large residuals; the OLS estimate is chosen to prevent
 large residuals for a few observations at the expense of tolerating relatively
 small residuals for many other observations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A sure-fire way of solving the minimization problem is to derive the first-order
 conditions by setting the partial derivatives equal to zero
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If 
\begin_inset Formula $h:\mathbb{R}^{k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 is a scalar-valued function of a 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-dimensional column vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-dimensional column vector whose 
\begin_inset Formula $k\mbox{-th}$
\end_inset

 element is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial h(x)}{\partial x_{k}}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $k\mbox{-th}$
\end_inset

 element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 This 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-dimensional column vector is called the 
\series bold
gradient
\series default
.
 Here, the 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and the function 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR\left(\tilde{\beta}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
SSR(\tilde{\beta}) & = & (y-X\tilde{\beta})^{\prime}(y-X\tilde{\beta})\\
 & = & (y^{\prime}-\tilde{\beta}^{\prime}X^{\prime})(y-X\tilde{\beta})\\
 & = & y^{\prime}y-\tilde{\beta}^{\prime}X^{\prime}y-y^{\prime}X\tilde{\beta}+\tilde{\beta}^{\prime}X^{\prime}X\tilde{\beta}\\
 & = & y^{\prime}y-2y^{\prime}X\tilde{\beta}+\tilde{\beta}^{\prime}X^{\prime}X\tilde{\beta}\ \ \mbox{(since \tilde{\beta}^{\prime}X^{\prime}y is a scalar and it equals its transpose y^{\prime}X\tilde{\beta})}\\
 & \equiv & y^{\prime}y-2\mathbf{a}^{\prime}\tilde{\beta}+\tilde{\beta}^{\prime}\mathbf{A}\tilde{\beta}\ \mbox{\ (with}\ \mathbf{a}\equiv X^{\prime}y\ \mbox{and}\ \mathbf{A}\equiv X^{\prime}X)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Recalling from matrix algebra that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial\left(\mathbf{a}^{\prime}\tilde{\beta}\right)}{\partial\tilde{\beta}}=\mathbf{a}\ \mbox{and}\ \frac{\partial\left(\tilde{\beta}^{\prime}\mathbf{A}\tilde{\beta}\right)}{\partial\tilde{\beta}}=2\mathbf{A}\tilde{\beta}\ \mbox{for \textbf{A} symmetric}
\]

\end_inset

the 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-dimensional column vector of partial derivatives is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial SSR\left(\tilde{\beta}\right)}{\partial\tilde{\beta}}=-2\mathbf{a}+2\mathbf{A}\tilde{\beta}
\]

\end_inset

The first-order conditions are obtained by setting the partial derivative
 above equal to zero.
 Recalling that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{a}\equiv X^{\prime}y\ \mbox{and}\ \mathbf{A}\equiv X^{\prime}X$
\end_inset

, we can write the first-order conditions as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
X^{\prime}X\hat{\beta}=X^{\prime}y\label{eq:4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here, we have replaced 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 because the OLS estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 that satisfies the first-order conditions.
 These 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 equations are called the 
\series bold
normal equations
\series default
.
 The 
\series bold
OLS predictor
\series default
, or the vector of 
\series bold
fitted values
\series default
, of the dependent variable is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{y}=X\hat{\beta}\label{eq:5}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The vector of residuals evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}=\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{u}\equiv y-X\hat{\beta}=y-\hat{y}\label{eq:6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is called the vector of 
\series bold
OLS residuals
\series default
.
 Its 
\begin_inset Formula $t\mbox{-th}$
\end_inset

 element is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{t}\equiv y_{t}-X_{t}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To be sure, the first-order conditions are just a necessary condition for
 minimization, so we have to check the second-order condition to make sure
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 achieves, the minimum.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial^{2}SSR\left(\tilde{\beta}\right)}{\partial\tilde{\beta}\partial\tilde{\beta}^{\prime}}=2X^{\prime}X>0
\]

\end_inset

The second-order condition is satisfied since 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}X$
\end_inset

 is positive definite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Finite-Sample Properties of the OLSE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 depends on the random sample 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,X)$
\end_inset

, then it is also random, and thus, it has statistical properties.
 Note, the characteristics of the distribution of the estimator we will
 derive are valid for any given sample size 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
 The OLSE is the 
\series bold
best linear unbiased estimator
\series default
 
\series bold
(BLUE)
\series default
 under certain assumptions.
 We present the minimum set of assumptions for each property of the OLSE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*

\bar under
Assumption 1
\bar default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $rank(X)=K\ \mbox{and}\ rank(X,y)=K+1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The assumption of 
\begin_inset Formula $rank(X)=K$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $N\geq K$
\end_inset

, and the columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 (observation vectors of each regressor) are linearly independent, i.e., none
 of the columns can be expressed as a linear combination of the other columns.
 This assumption is called the assumption of full column rank.
 The regressors are said to be 
\series bold
(perfectly) multicollinear
\series default
 if the assumption is not satisfied.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The assumption of 
\begin_inset Formula $rank(X,y)=K+1$
\end_inset

 implies that there is no exact solution for the unknown coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 in a linear equation 
\begin_inset Formula $y=X\beta$
\end_inset

.
 If this rank is 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is linearly dependent on the columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 and we can find a nonzero vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y={\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{K}}\beta_{k}X_{k}=\beta_{1}\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{11}\\
\vdots\\
x_{t1}
\end{array}\right]+\ldots+\beta_{k}\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{1k}\\
\vdots\\
x_{tk}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Under these assumptions, the OLSE has the following properties:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
\emph on
unique
\series default
\emph default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This holds because 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}X$
\end_inset

 is positive definite and nonsingular due to the assumption of full column
 rank.
 The normal equations can be solved uniquely for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 by premultiplying both sides by 
\begin_inset Formula $(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y\label{eq:7}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Viewed as a function of the sample 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,X)$
\end_inset

, this is often called the 
\series bold
OLS estimator
\series default
.
 For any given sample 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,X)$
\end_inset

, the value of this function is the 
\series bold
OLS estimate
\series default
.
 Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{k}$
\end_inset

 is an estimate of the unknown population parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The predicted value of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 can now be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}=X\hat{\beta}=X(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y=Py$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $P\equiv X(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times N$
\end_inset

.
 The projection matrix, 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

, projects 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 onto the column space of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 is symmetric and idempotent, and 
\begin_inset Formula $PX=X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $rank(X)<K$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}X$
\end_inset

 is positive semi-definite and singular, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is not unique because an infinite number of solutions exist.
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\series bold
sampling error
\series default
 is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}-\beta$
\end_inset

.
 It can be related to 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{\beta}-\beta & = & (X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y-\beta\ \mbox{(by (\ref{eq:7}))}\nonumber \\
 & = & (X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}(X\beta+u)-\beta\ \ \mbox{(since}\ y=X\beta+u)\nonumber \\
 & = & \beta+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}u-\beta\nonumber \\
 & = & (X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}u=Au\ \ (\mbox{where}\ A\equiv(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}\mbox{)}\label{eq:8}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(b) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
linear
\emph default
 estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset


\series default
 is a linear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\hat{\beta}_{k}=b_{k1}y_{1}+\cdots+b_{kN}y_{N},\ k=1,\ldots,K\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\begin{array}{c}
\hat{\beta}_{1}\\
\hat{\beta}_{2}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
b_{11} & b_{12} & \cdots & b_{1N}\\
b_{21} & b_{22} & \cdots & b_{2N}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
y_{1}\\
\vdots\\
y_{N}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
b_{11}y_{1}+\cdots+b_{1N}y_{N}\\
b_{21}y_{1}+\cdots+b_{2N}y_{N}
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
where 
\begin_inset Formula $(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
b_{11} & b_{12} & \cdots & b_{1N}\\
b_{21} & b_{22} & \cdots & b_{2N}
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(c) The OLS regression residual, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note, Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 it is a random vector, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset

 should not be considered an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Recall, the vector of OLS regression residuals is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}\equiv y-X\hat{\beta}=y-\hat{y}=\left[I_{N}-X(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X\right]y=My$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $M\equiv\left[I_{N}-X(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X\right]$
\end_inset

.
 The annihilator matrix, 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

, is a symmetric and idempotent matrix with rank 
\begin_inset Formula $N-K$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $MX=0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(c.1)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}=My=M(X\beta+u)=MX\beta+Mu=Mu$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(c.2)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}\hat{u}=X^{\prime}Mu=(MX)^{\prime}u=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset

 is orthogonal to the columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}\hat{u}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
x_{11} & \cdots & x_{1N}\\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
x_{K1} & \cdots & x_{KN}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\hat{u}_{1}\\
\vdots\\
\hat{u}_{N}
\end{array}\right]\implies x_{k1}\hat{u}_{1}+\cdots+x_{kN}\hat{u}_{N}={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}X_{tk}\hat{u}_{t}=X_{k}^{\prime}\hat{u}=0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This shows that the normal equations can be interpreted as the sample analogue
 of the orthogonality conditions 
\begin_inset Formula $E(X_{t}^{\prime}\cdot u_{t})=0$
\end_inset

, which will be covered in the next subsection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(c.3)
\series default
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 have a column of a constant, so 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t1}=1,\ \forall\ t=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

.
 From the result in 
\series bold
(c.2)
\series default
, 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}X_{t1}\hat{u}_{t}=0\iff{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\hat{u}=0\iff n^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\hat{u}=0\iff\bar{\hat{u}}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a result, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}=X\hat{\beta}\implies y=X\hat{\beta}+\hat{u}\implies\bar{y}=\bar{X}\hat{\beta}+\bar{\hat{u}}=\bar{X}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To sum up, if 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 has a column of a constant, then 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\hat{u}=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}=\bar{X}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset

 are the samples means of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 That is, the regression line passes through the point of sample means.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 does not have a column of a constant, the regression line does not pass
 through the point of sample means.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*

\emph on
\bar under
Assumption 2
\emph default
\bar default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u|X)=0$
\end_inset

 (Strict Exogeneity)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This assumption implies that the regressors are strictly exogeneous.
 So, the expectiation (mean) is conditional on the regressors for 
\emph on
all
\emph default
 observations, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{t}|X_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,X_{N}^{\prime})=0\ \ (t=1,\ldots,N).\label{eq:9}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

To state the assumption differently, take, for any given observation 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, the joint distribution of the 
\begin_inset Formula $nK+1$
\end_inset

 random variables, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(u_{t},X_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,X_{N}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, and consider the conditional distribution, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(u_{t}|X_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,X_{N}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 The conditional mean 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}|X_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,X_{N}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is, in general, a nonlinear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $(X_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,X_{N}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 The strict exogeneity assumption says that this function is a constant
 value of zero.
 It also implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|X)=X\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Note, if 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a fixed variate, then 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is strongly exogeneous.
 Weak exogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 requires only 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}|X_{t}^{\prime})=0\ \forall\ t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assuming this constant to be zero is not restrictive if the regressors include
 a constant because the equation can be rewritten so that the conditional
 mean of the eror is zero.
 To see this, suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}|X)=\mu$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t1}=1$
\end_inset

.
 The equation can be written at
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{t} & = & \beta_{1}+\beta_{2}x_{t2}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{tK}+u_{t}\\
 & = & (\beta_{1}+\mu)+\beta_{2}x_{t2}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{tK}+(u_{t}-\mu)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

If we redefine 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}+\mu$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 to be 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}-\mu$
\end_inset

, the conditional mean of the new error term is zero.
 In virtually all applications, the regressors include a constant term.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Implications of Strict Exogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The unconditional mean of the error term is zero, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{t})=0\ \ (t=1,\ldots,N)\label{eq:10}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is because, by the Law of Total Expectations
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The Law of Total Expectiations states that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[E\left(y|\mathbf{x}\right)\right]=E(y)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[E(u_{t}|X)\right]=E(u_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Under strict exogeneity, the regressors are orthogonal to the error term
 for 
\emph on
all 
\emph default
observations, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(x_{sk}u_{t})=0\ \ (s,t=1,\ldots,N;\ k=1,\ldots,K)
\]

\end_inset

 or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(X_{s}^{\prime}u_{t})=\left[\begin{array}{c}
E(x_{s1}u_{t})\\
\vdots\\
E(x_{sK}u_{t})
\end{array}\right]=\mathbf{0}_{(K\times1)}\ \ (\forall\ t,s)\label{eq:11}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{sk}$
\end_inset

 is an element of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, strict exogeneity implies 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}|x_{sk})=E\left[E(u_{t}|X)|x_{sk}\right]=0$
\end_inset

 by the Law of Iterated Expectiations.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The Law of Iterated Expectations states that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[E\left(y|\mathbf{x},\mathbf{z}\right)|\mathbf{x}\right]=E(y|\mathbf{x})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 It follows from this that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(x_{sk}u_{t}) & = & E\left[E(x_{sk}u_{t}|x_{sk})\right]\ \mbox{(by the Law of Total Expectiations)}\\
 & = & E[x_{sk}E(u_{t}|x_{sk})]\ \mbox{(by the linearity of conditional expectiations)}\\
 & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

 Strict exogeneity requires the regressors be orthogonal not only to the
 error term from the same observation (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{tk}u_{t})=0\ \forall\ k$
\end_inset

), but also to the error term from the other observations (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{sk}u_{t})\ \forall\ k\ \mbox{and for}\ s\neq t$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Because the mean of the error term is zero, the orthogonality conditions
 are equivalent to zero-correlation conditions.
 This is because
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(u_{t},x_{sk}) & = & E(x_{sk}u_{t})-E(x_{sk})E(u_{t})\ \ \mbox{(by definition of covariance)}\\
 & = & E(x_{sk}u_{t})\ \ \mbox{(by\ (\ref{eq:10}))}\\
 & = & 0\ \ \mbox{(by the orthogonality conditions (\ref{eq:11}))}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

In particular, for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=s$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(x_{tk},u_{t})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, strict exogeneity implies the requirement that the regressors
 be contemporaneously uncorreclated with the error term.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(d) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
unbiased
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(\hat{\beta}|X\right)=\beta\ \forall\ \beta$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Box Frameless
position "t"
hor_pos "c"
has_inner_box 1
inner_pos "t"
use_parbox 0
use_makebox 0
width "100col%"
special "none"
height "1in"
height_special "totalheight"
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left(\hat{\beta}|X\right) & = & E\left(\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}y|X\right)\ \ \mbox{(by (\ref{eq:7}))}\\
 & = & E\left(\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}\left(X\beta+u\right)|X\right)\ \mbox{\ (since}\ y=X\beta+u)\\
 & = & E\left(\beta+\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}u|X\right)\\
 & = & E\left(\beta|X\right)+E\left(\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}u|X\right)\ \mbox{\ (by the linearity of conditional expectations)}\\
 & = & \beta+\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}E(u|X)\ \mbox{\ (since}\ \beta\ \mbox{is a constant and}\ \left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}\ \mbox{is a function of \ensuremath{X})}\\
 & = & \beta\ \mbox{\ (by (\ref{eq:9}))}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The OLS estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is a function of the sample 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,X)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,X)$
\end_inset

 are random, so is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 Now imagine that we fix 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 at some given value, calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 for all samples corresponding to all possible realizations of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, and take the average of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 This average is the (population) conditional mean 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $E\left(\hat{\beta}|X\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The result above says that this average equals the true value 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 It should be emphasized that the strict exogeneity assumption is critical
 for proving unbiasedness.
 Anything short of strict exogeneity will not do.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There is another notion of unbiasedness that is weaker than the unbiasedness
 above.
 The result above implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(\hat{\beta}\right)=E\left[E\left(\hat{\beta}|X\right)\right]=\beta$
\end_inset

, by the Law of Total Expectations.
 This says that if we calculated 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 for all possible different samples, differeing not only in 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 but also in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, the average would be the true value.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is the best estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 in that it minimizes the 
\series bold
mean squared error (MSE)
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\left(\hat{\beta}-\beta\right)^{2}\right]$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(e) Linear parametric functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 be a 
\begin_inset Formula $J\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix of nonzero constants.
 An unbiased estimator of linearly estimable linear parametric functions
 
\begin_inset Formula $R\beta$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(f) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset

 is unbiased
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The residual 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset

 is an unbiased estimator of the error term 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 in the sense that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(\hat{u}-u|X\right)=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*

\emph on
\bar under
Assumption 3
\emph default
\bar default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $E(uu^{\prime}|X)=\sigma^{2}I_{N}$
\end_inset

 (Spherical error covariance)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This assumption implies that the error terms 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{t})$
\end_inset

 are homoskedastic (have the same variance) and uncorrelated with each other
 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(u_{t}^{2}|X) & = & \sigma^{2}>0\ \ (t=1,2,\ldots,N)\label{eq:12}\\
E(u_{t}u_{s}|X) & = & 0\ \ (t,s=1,2,\ldots,N;\ t\neq s)\label{eq:13}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

The homoskedasticity assumption (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:12"

\end_inset

) says that the conditional second moment, which in general is a nonlinear
 function of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, is a constant.
 Thanks to strict exogeneity, this condition can be stated equivalently
 in more familiar terms.
 Consider the conditional variance 
\begin_inset Formula $var(u_{t}|X)$
\end_inset

.
 It equals the same constant because
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
var(u_{t}|X) & = & E(u_{t}^{2}|X)-E(u_{t}|X)^{2}\ \ \mbox{(by the definition of conditional variance)}\\
 & = & E(u_{t}^{2}|X)\ \ \mbox{(by strict exogeneity)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Similarly, (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:13"

\end_inset

) is equivalent to the requirement that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
cov(u_{t},u_{s}|X)=0\ \ (t,s=1,2,\ldots,N;\ t\neq s)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is, in the joint distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{t},u_{s})$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, the covariance is zero.
 In the context of time-series models, (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:13"

\end_inset

) states that there is no 
\series bold
serial correlation
\series default
 in the error term.
 The discussion above shows that the assumption can also be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
cov(u|X)=\sigma^{2}I_{N}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With the third assumption added to the previous two, we have the following
 key results for the OLSE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(g) The covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $V\equiv cov(\hat{\beta}|X)=\sigma^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Box Frameless
position "t"
hor_pos "c"
has_inner_box 1
inner_pos "t"
use_parbox 0
use_makebox 0
width "100col%"
special "none"
height "1in"
height_special "totalheight"
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(\hat{\beta}|X) & = & cov(\hat{\beta}-\beta|X)\ \ \mbox{(since \ensuremath{\beta}is not random)}\\
 & = & cov(Au|X)\ \ \mbox{(by (\ref{eq:8}))}\\
 & = & Acov(u|X)A^{\prime}\ \ \mbox{(since A is a function of \ensuremath{X})}\\
 & = & A\left(\sigma^{2}I_{N}\right)A^{\prime}\ \ \mbox{(since}\ cov(u|X)=\sigma^{2}I_{N}\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}AA^{\prime}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}\ \ \mbox{(since}\ AA^{\prime}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}X(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}\mbox{)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(R\hat{\beta}|X)=Rcov(R\hat{\beta}|X)R^{\prime}=\sigma^{2}R(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}R^{\prime}=RVR^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for a matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 of constants.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(h) Gauss-Markov Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The OLSE is 
\series bold
efficient
\series default
 in the class of linear unbiased estimators.
 That is, for any unbiased estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 that is linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\tilde{\beta}|X)\geq cov(\hat{\beta}|X)$
\end_inset

 in the matrix sense, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\tilde{\beta}|X)-cov(\hat{\beta}|X)$
\end_inset

 is positive semi-definite.
 So,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a^{\prime}\left[cov(\tilde{\beta}|X)-cov(\hat{\beta}|X)\right]a\ge0
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a^{\prime}\left[cov(\tilde{\beta}|X)\right]a\ge a^{\prime}\left[cov(\hat{\beta}|X)\right]a
\]

\end_inset

for any 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-dimensional vector 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, consider a special vector whose elements are all 0 except
 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th element, which is 1.
 For this particular 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

, the quadratic form above picks up the 
\begin_inset Formula $(k,k)$
\end_inset

 element, which is 
\begin_inset Formula $var(\tilde{\beta}_{k}|X)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}_{k}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the matrix inequality above implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
var(\tilde{\beta}_{k}|X)\ge var(\hat{\beta}_{k}|X)\ \ (k=1,2,\ldots,K)\label{eq:14}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

That is, for any regression coefficient, the variance of the OLS estimator
 is no larger than that of any other linear unbiased estimator.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:14"

\end_inset

), along with the unbiasedness of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

, imply
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
var(\tilde{\beta})\ge var(\hat{\beta})\ \ \mbox{(where \ensuremath{\tilde{\beta}}\ is any linear unbiased estimator)}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Gauss-Markov says that the OLSE is 
\series bold
efficient
\series default
 in the sense that its conditional covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{cov}(\hat{\beta}|X)$
\end_inset

 is smallest among linear unbiased estimators.
 For this reason, the OLSE is called the Best Linear Unbiased Estimator
 
\series bold
(BLUE)
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, then it can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}=Cy$
\end_inset

 for some matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

, which is possibly a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $D\equiv C-A$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $C=D+A$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $A=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\tilde{\beta} & = & (D+A)y\\
 & = & Dy+Ay\\
 & = & D(X\beta+u)+\hat{\beta}\ \ \mbox{(since}\ y=X\beta+u\ \mbox{and}\ \hat{\beta}=Ay\mbox{)}\\
 & = & DX\beta+Du+\hat{\beta}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Take the conditional expectations on both sides, we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(\tilde{\beta}|X) & = & E(DX\beta+Du+\hat{\beta}|X)\\
 & = & DX\beta+DE(u|X)+\beta\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \hat{\beta}\ \mbox{is unbiased)}\\
 & = & DX\beta+\beta\ \ \mbox{(since}\ E(u|X)=0\mbox{)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is unbiased if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $DX\beta=0$
\end_inset

.
 For this to be true for any given 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, it is necessary that 
\begin_inset Formula $DX=0$
\end_inset

.
 So 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}=Du+\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\tilde{\beta}-\beta & = & Du+(\hat{\beta}-\beta)\\
 & = & (D+A)u\ \ \mbox{(by (\ref{eq:8}))}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(\tilde{\beta}|X) & = & cov(\tilde{\beta}-\beta|X)\\
 & = & cov((D+A)u|X)\\
 & = & (D+A)cov(u|X)(D+A)^{\prime}\ \ \mbox{(since \ensuremath{D}\ and \ensuremath{A}\ are functions of X)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}\cdot(D+A)(D^{\prime}+A^{\prime})\ \ \mbox{(since}\ cov(u|X)=\sigma^{2}I_{N}\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}\cdot\left(DD^{\prime}+DA^{\prime}+AD^{\prime}+AA^{\prime}\right)\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}\cdot(DD^{\prime}+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1})\\
 &  & (DA^{\prime}=DX(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}=0\ \mbox{since}\ DX=0\ \mbox{and}\ AA^{\prime}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}\mbox{)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(\tilde{\beta}|X) & = & \sigma^{2}\cdot\left[DD^{\prime}+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}\mbox{)}\right]\ge\sigma^{2}\cdot(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}=cov(\hat{\beta}|X)\\
 &  & \mbox{(since}\ DD^{\prime}\ \mbox{is positive semi-definite)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(i) BLUE of linear parametric functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The unique BLUE of linearly estimable linear parametric functions 
\begin_inset Formula $R\beta$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be as defined above.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(R\tilde{\beta})-cov(R\hat{\beta}) & = & \sigma^{2}R(DD^{\prime}+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1})R^{\prime}-\sigma^{2}R[(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}]R^{\prime}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}R[DD^{\prime}+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}-(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}]R^{\prime}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}R[DD^{\prime}]R^{\prime}\ge0\\
 &  & \mbox{(since}\ DD^{\prime}\ \mbox{is positive semi-definite)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(j) Conditional covariance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\hat{u}|X)=\sigma^{2}M$
\end_inset

, which is singluar.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{s}$
\end_inset

 are correlated 
\begin_inset Formula $(cov(\hat{u}_{t},\hat{u}_{s}|X_{s}^{\prime})\neq0)$
\end_inset

 even if we assmed that the true error terms are uncorrelated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(\hat{u}|X) & = & cov(Mu|X)M\\
 & = & cov(Mu|X)\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \hat{u}=Mu\mbox{)}\\
 & = & Mcov(u|X)M^{\prime}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}MM^{\prime}\ \ \mbox{(since}\ cov(u|X)=\sigma^{2}I_{N}\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}M\ \ \mbox{(since \ensuremath{M^{\prime}=M}\ and \ensuremath{MM=M})}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(k) Estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let the LSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 be
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{SSR}{N-K}=\frac{\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}}{N-K}\label{eq:15}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The intuitive reason to dvide the SSR by 
\begin_inset Formula $N-K$
\end_inset

 rather than by 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is that 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta)$
\end_inset

 have to be estimated before obtaining the residual vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset

 used to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 More specifically, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset

 has to satisfy the 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 normal equations (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:4"

\end_inset

), which limits the variability of the residual.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is an unbased estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}}{N-K}$
\end_inset

 the proof amounts to showing that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}|X)=(N-K)\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}|X) & = & E[(Mu)^{\prime}(Mu)|X]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \hat{u}=Mu\mbox{)}\\
 & = & E[u^{\prime}Mu|X]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ M^{\prime}=M\ \mbox{and}\ MM=M\mbox{)}\\
 & = & E[tr(u^{\prime}Mu)|X]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ u^{\prime}Mu\ \mbox{is a scalar)}\\
 & = & E[tr(Muu^{\prime})|X]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ tr(ABC)=tr(CAB)\mbox{)}\\
 & = & tr[E(Muu^{\prime}|X)]\ \ \mbox{(since trace is a linear operator)}\\
 & = & tr[ME(uu^{\prime}|X)]\ \ \mbox{(since \ensuremath{M}\ is a function of \ensuremath{X})}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}tr(M)\ \ \mbox{(since}\ E(uu^{\prime}|X)=\sigma^{2}I_{N}\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}tr(I_{N}-X(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime})\ \ \mbox{(since}\ M=I_{N}-X(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}[tr(I_{N})-tr(X(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime})]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ tr(A+B)=tr(A)+tr(B)\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}[tr(I_{N})-tr((X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}X)]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ tr(ABC)=tr(CAB)\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}[tr(I_{N})-tr(I_{K})]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ X^{\prime}X:K\times K\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}(N-K)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the unbiased estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\hat{\beta}|X)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}\equiv\hat{\sigma}^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}$
\end_inset

, and the unbiased estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(R\hat{\beta}|X)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{V}R^{\prime}=\hat{\sigma}^{2}R(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}R^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The square root of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}$
\end_inset

, is called the 
\series bold
standard error of the regression (SER)
\series default
 or 
\series bold
standard error of the equation (SEE)
\series default
.
 It is an estimate of the standard deviation of the error term.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*

\emph on
\bar under
Assumption 4
\emph default
\bar default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $(u|X)$
\end_inset

 is distributed as a multivariate normal 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,\sigma^{2}I)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
An 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-dimensional random vector 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is distributed as a multivariate normal, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\sim N(\mu,\Sigma)$
\end_inset

, with a mean vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 and a nonsingular (positive definite) variance-covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma$
\end_inset

, if the joint density function is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y)=(2\pi)^{-\frac{n}{2}}|\Sigma|^{-\frac{1}{2}}\exp\left\{ \frac{-(y-\mu)^{\prime}\Sigma^{-1}(y-\mu)}{2}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 be an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-dimensional normal variate: 
\begin_inset Formula $y\sim N(\mu,\Sigma)$
\end_inset

.
 Then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $(Ay-b)\sim N((A\mu-b),A\Sigma A^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is an 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times n$
\end_inset

 matrix of constants with 
\begin_inset Formula $rank(A)=m\le n$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 is an 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

-dimensional vector of constants.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(b) Let 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{\prime}P=\Sigma^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 There exists such a nonsingular matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 for a positive definite matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y-\mu)$
\end_inset

 is distributed as a standed multivariate normal: 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,I)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, under Assumption 4, we can specify the distribution functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We need to know the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 because it drives the statistical properties of the estimators.
 When we know the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, we know the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 We will then be able to formulate statistical tests, or inferences, of
 hypotheses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assumption 4 implies that the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a multivariate normal 
\begin_inset Formula $N(X\beta,\sigma^{2}I)$
\end_inset

.
 This is because a linear transformation of a multivariate normal random
 vector is still a multivariate normal random vector by Theorem 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(l) Distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}\sim N(\beta,V)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}\sim N(0,\sigma^{2}M)$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have already derived the mean and covariance matrix of both random vectors.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is linear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset

 is a linear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}=Mu$
\end_inset

, then the results of Theorem 1 hold.
 So, both random vectors are multivariate normal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(m) Distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 be an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-dimensional normal variate: 
\begin_inset Formula $y\sim N(\mu,\Sigma)$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $(y-\mu)^{\prime}\Sigma^{-1}(y-\mu)$
\end_inset

 is distributed as 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}(n)$
\end_inset

, a central chi-square random variable with 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 degrees of freedom.
 Note, from (b) in the previous Theorem, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $z=P(y-\mu)\sim N(0,I)$
\end_inset

, that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 are i.i.d.
 standard normal random variables.
 The quadratic function can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(y-\mu)^{\prime}\Sigma^{-1}(y-\mu)=(y-\mu)^{\prime}P^{\prime}P(y-\mu)=z^{\prime}z={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}
\]

\end_inset

which is the sum of squared independent standard normal random variables.
 Thus, we can define a 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 random variable with 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 degrees of freedom as the sum of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 squared independent standard normal random variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(b) 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\Sigma^{-1}y$
\end_inset

 is distributed as 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}(n;\delta)$
\end_inset

, a noncentral chi-square distribution with 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 degrees of freesom and the noncentrality parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(c) 
\begin_inset Formula $(y-b)^{\prime}\Sigma^{-1}(y-b)$
\end_inset

 is distributed as 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}(n;\delta)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta^{2}=(\mu-b)^{\prime}\Sigma^{-1}(\mu-b)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(d) If 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma=\sigma^{2}I$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{y^{\prime}Ay}{\sigma^{2}}$
\end_inset

 is distributed as 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}(m;\delta)$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is an 
\series bold
idempotent matrix
\series default
 of rank 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

, where the noncentrality parameter is 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta^{2}=\frac{\mu^{\prime}A\mu}{\sigma^{2}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(e) Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 be an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

- dimensional (not necessarily normal) random vector with 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y)=\mu$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(y)=\Sigma$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y^{\prime}Ay)=tr(A\Sigma)+\mu^{\prime}A\mu$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $W\equiv(N-K)\frac{\hat{\sigma}^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}=\frac{\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}}{\sigma^{2}}=\frac{u^{\prime}Mu}{\sigma^{2}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u\sim N(0,\sigma^{2}I)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is idempotent, and 
\begin_inset Formula $rank(M)=N-K$
\end_inset

, then by part (d) of Theorem 2, 
\begin_inset Formula $W=\frac{u^{\prime}Mu}{\sigma^{2}}\sim\chi^{2}(N-K)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(n) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y\sim N(\mu,\Sigma)$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(a) A linear function 
\begin_inset Formula $By$
\end_inset

 and a quadratic function 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}Ay$
\end_inset

 are independent if 
\begin_inset Formula $B\Sigma A=0$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is a symmetric matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(b) Two quadratic functions 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}Ay$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}By$
\end_inset

 are independent if 
\begin_inset Formula $A\Sigma B=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}=\beta+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}-\beta=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}u$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}=(N-K)^{-1}\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}=(N-K)^{-1}u^{\prime}Mu$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $B=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime},\ A=M,\ \Sigma=\sigma^{2}I$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset Formula $B\Sigma A=\sigma^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}M=\sigma^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}(MX)^{\prime}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
By part (a) from Theorem 3 above, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Distributions Used in Statistical Inference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\sim N(\mu,1),\ Z\sim\chi^{2}(n;\delta),\ \mbox{and}\ W\sim\chi^{2}(m)$
\end_inset

 which are independent.
 Then,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{Y}{\sqrt{\frac{W}{m}}}\sim t(m;\mu)$
\end_inset

, noncentral 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(b) 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\nicefrac{Z}{n}}{\nicefrac{W}{m}}\sim F(n,m;\delta)$
\end_inset

, noncentral 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 be a 
\begin_inset Formula $J\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix of known constants with rank 
\begin_inset Formula $J\times K$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 be a 
\begin_inset Formula $J\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of known constants.
 Then results in 
\series bold
(l)-(n)
\series default
 imply
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(o) 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}\sim N(R\beta,RVR^{\prime})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(p) 
\begin_inset Formula $Z^{*}\equiv(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)^{\prime}(RVR^{\prime})^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)\sim\chi^{2}(J)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(q) 
\begin_inset Formula $Z\equiv(R\hat{\beta}-q)^{\prime}(RVR^{\prime})^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)\sim\chi^{2}(J;\delta)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(r) Central F distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Deriving the F test:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
F^{*} & \equiv & \frac{\nicefrac{Z^{*}}{J}}{\nicefrac{W}{(N-K)}}\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{1}{J}\frac{(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)^{\prime}(RVR^{\prime})^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)}{\nicefrac{\left(\nicefrac{(N-K)\hat{\sigma}^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}\right)}{(N-K)}}\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{\sigma^{2}}{J\sigma^{2}}\frac{(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)^{\prime}(R(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}R^{\prime})^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)}{\hat{\sigma}^{2}}\nonumber \\
F^{*} & = & \frac{1}{J}(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)^{\prime}(R\hat{V}R^{\prime})^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)\sim F(J,N-K)\label{eq:16}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(s) Noncentral F distribution
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F\equiv\frac{\nicefrac{Z}{J}}{\nicefrac{W}{(N-K)}}=\frac{1}{J}(R\hat{\beta}-q)^{\prime}(R\hat{V}R^{\prime})^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)\sim F(J,N-K;\delta)\label{eq:17}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(t) Central and noncentral t distributions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the number of restrictions is 1, we need to consider the following
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-statistic.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{1}\sim N(\mu,1)\ \mbox{and}\ Z_{2}\sim\chi^{2}(m)$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{2}$
\end_inset

 are independent, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t=\frac{Z_{1}}{\sqrt{\nicefrac{Z_{2}}{m}}}\sim t(m;\mu)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 is the noncentrality parameter which can be either positive, zero, or negative.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=0$
\end_inset

, it is a central 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $J=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is a scalar, its true variance and estimated variance are also scalars.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
t^{*} & = & \frac{\nicefrac{(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)}{sd(R\hat{\beta})}}{\sqrt{\nicefrac{W}{(N-K)}}}=\frac{\nicefrac{(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)}{sd(R\hat{\beta})}}{\sqrt{\nicefrac{\hat{\sigma}^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}}}=\frac{(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta)}{est.sd(R\hat{\beta})}\sim t(N-K)\label{eq:18}\\
t & = & \frac{(R\hat{\beta}-q)}{est.sd(R\hat{\beta})}\sim t(N-K;R\beta-q)\label{eq:19}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Remark: It is easy to verfiy the following relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 distribution and 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 distribution:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t(N-K)^{2}\sim F(1,N-K)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Consider a row vector 
\begin_inset Formula $R=(0,0,\ldots,0,1,0,\ldots,0)$
\end_inset

 which has 1 in the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th position.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}=\hat{\beta}_{k}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{V}R^{\prime}=\hat{v}_{kk}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th diagonal element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}$
\end_inset

, which is the estimate of the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
t^{*} & = & \frac{\hat{\beta}_{k}-\beta_{k}}{\sqrt{\hat{v}_{kk}}}\sim t(N-K)\\
t & = & \frac{\hat{\beta}_{k}-q}{\sqrt{\hat{v}_{kk}}}\sim t(N-K;\beta_{k}-q)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

For another example, consider a row vector 
\begin_inset Formula $R=(a,b,0,\ldots,0)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 are constants.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}=a\hat{\beta}_{1}+b\hat{\beta}_{2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{V}R^{\prime}=a^{2}\hat{v}_{11}+b^{2}\hat{v}_{22}+2ab\hat{v}_{12}$
\end_inset

, which is the estimated variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

, and hence
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
t^{*} & = & \frac{(a\hat{\beta}_{1}+b\hat{\beta}_{2})-(a\beta_{1}+b\beta_{2})}{est.sd(a\hat{\beta}_{1}+b\hat{\beta}_{2})}\sim t(N-K)\\
t & = & \frac{(a\hat{\beta}_{1}+b\hat{\beta}_{2})-q}{est.sd(a\hat{\beta}_{1}+b\hat{\beta}_{2})}\sim t(N-K;a\beta_{1}+b\beta_{2}-q)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The Classical Regression Model for Random Samples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The sample 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,X)$
\end_inset

 is a 
\series bold
random sample
\series default
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\{y_{t},X_{t}\}$
\end_inset

 is i.i.d.
 (independent and identically distributed) across observations.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{t},X_{t})$
\end_inset

 by Assumption 1 and since 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{t},X_{t})$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{s},X_{s})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t\neq s$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{t},X_{t})$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{s}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t\neq s$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(u_{t}|X) & = & E(u_{t}|X_{t}^{\prime})\nonumber \\
E(u_{t}^{2}|X) & = & E(u_{t}^{2}|X_{t}^{\prime})\nonumber \\
E(u_{t}u_{s}|X) & = & E(u_{t}|X_{t}^{\prime})E(u_{s}|X_{s}^{\prime})\ \ \mbox{(for}\ t\neq s\mbox{)}\label{eq:20}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Therefore, Assumptions (A2) and (A3) reduce to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
(A2):E(u_{t}|X_{t}^{\prime}) & = & 0\ \ (t=1,2,\ldots,N)\label{eq:21}\\
(A3):E(u_{t}^{2}|X_{t}^{\prime}) & = & \sigma^{2}>0\ \ (t=1,2,\ldots,N)\label{eq:22}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The implication of the identical distribution aspect of a random sample
 is that the joint distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{t},X_{t})$
\end_inset

 does not depened on 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 So the unconditional second moment 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}^{2})$
\end_inset

 is constant across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 (this is referred to as 
\series bold
unconditional homoskedasticity
\series default
) and the functional form of the conditional second moment 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}^{2}|X_{t}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is the same across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 However, (A3), that the 
\emph on
value
\emph default
 of the conditional second moment is the same across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, does not follow.
 Therefore, (A3) remains restricitive for the case of a random sample; without
 it, the conditional second moment 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}^{2}|X_{t}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 can differ across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 through its possible dependence on 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 To emphasize the distinction, the restictions on the conditional second
 moments are referred to as 
\series bold
conditional homoskedasticity
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Measurement of Goodness of Fit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

How well do the regressors explain the dependent variable? How do we measure
 the goodness of the regression? A measure of the goodness of fit is the
 squared correlation coefficient between the dependent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 and the least squares predictor 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{t}=X_{t}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R^{2}\equiv[corr(y_{t},\hat{y}_{t})]^{2}=\frac{[cov(y_{t},\hat{y}_{t})]^{2}}{var(y_{t})var(\hat{y}_{t})}=\frac{\left[\nicefrac{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{\hat{y}})}{N}\right]^{2}}{\left[\nicefrac{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})}{N}\right]\left[\nicefrac{(\hat{y}-\bar{\hat{y}})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{\hat{y}})}{N}\right]}\label{eq:23}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\hat{y}}$
\end_inset

 are the vectors of sample means of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{t}$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is a squared correlation coefficient, it akes a value between 0 and 1 
\begin_inset Formula $(0\le R^{2}\leq1)$
\end_inset

, and its value is independent of the measurement unit.
 A high 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is interpreted as a good fit and the regressors explain the variation of
 the dependentvariable well.
 This expression can be simplified further by using the following results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
When a constant is one of the regressors,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}=\bar{\hat{y}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
(b) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
(c) 
\begin_inset Formula $(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})=(\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
(d) 
\begin_inset Formula $(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})=(\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})+\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let a constant be one of the regressors, then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}=\bar{X}\hat{\beta}=n^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}X_{t}\hat{\beta}=n^{-1}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\hat{y}_{t}=\bar{\hat{y}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
(b) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})=\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{y}-\hat{u}^{\prime}\bar{y}=(Mu)^{\prime}(X\hat{\beta})-{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}\hat{u}_{t}\bar{y}=0-\bar{y}\sum_{t=1}^{N}\hat{u}_{t}=0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
(c) 
\begin_inset Formula $(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})=(\left[\hat{y}-\bar{y}\right]+\hat{u})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})=(\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})+\hat{u}^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})=(\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
(d)
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
 
\begin_inset Formula $(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})=([\hat{y}-\bar{y}]+\hat{u})^{\prime}([\hat{y}-\bar{y}]+\hat{u})=(\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})+\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we substitute these results into the equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
R^{2} & = & \frac{\left[\nicefrac{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{\hat{y}})}{N}\right]^{2}}{\left[\nicefrac{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})}{N}\right]\left[\nicefrac{(\hat{y}-\bar{\hat{y}})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{\hat{y}})}{N}\right]}\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{[(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})]^{2}}{[(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})][(\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})]}\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \bar{y}=\bar{\hat{y}}\mbox{)}\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{[(\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})]^{2}}{[(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})][(\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})]}\ \ \mbox{(since}\ (y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})=(\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})\mbox{)}\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{(\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})}{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})}\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})}{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})}-\frac{\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}}{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})}\ \ \mbox{(since}\ (\hat{y}-\bar{y})^{\prime}(\hat{y}-\bar{y})=(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})-\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}\mbox{)}\nonumber \\
 & = & 1-\frac{\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}}{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})}\label{eq:24}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

This is the expression more commonly used to define 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

, which is called the 
\series bold
coefficient of (multiple) determination
\series default
.
 The denominator term is called the 
\series bold
total sum of squares (TSS)
\series default
 and is a measure of the variation of the dependent variable.
 The numerator in the first expression is called the 
\series bold
explained sum of squares
\series default
 or 
\series bold
regression sum of squares (RSS)
\series default
, and the SSR, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}$
\end_inset

, is also called the 
\series bold
unexplained sum of squares
\series default
 or 
\series bold
error sum of squares (ESS)
\series default
.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R^{2}=\frac{RSS}{TSS}=1-\frac{ESS}{TSS}\label{eq:25}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the estimation equation does not include an intercept term, and you compute
 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 by the second expression, then the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be negative.
 With an intercept of 0, the OLS residuals no longer sum to zero, so most
 of the properties in the Lemma above fail to hold.
 We can see this by the following derivation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
ESS & = & \hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}\\
 & = & (y-\hat{y})^{\prime}(y-\hat{y})\\
 & = & [(y-\bar{y})+(\bar{y}-\hat{y})]^{\prime}[(y-\bar{y})+(\bar{y}-\hat{y})]\\
 & = & (y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})-2(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})+(\bar{y}-\hat{y})^{\prime}(\bar{y}-\hat{y})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

This implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
R^{2}=\frac{-2(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})+(\bar{y}-\hat{y})^{\prime}(\bar{y}-\hat{y})}{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another serious drawback of using 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 as a measure of goodness of fit is that it is a nondecreasing, and typically
 an increasing, function of the number of regressors.
 To explain, 
\begin_inset Formula $TSS=RSS+ESS$
\end_inset

, so minimizing the 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR$
\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset Formula $ESS$
\end_inset

, is equivalent to maximizing 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 When an additional variable is included, 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 improves if the coefficient on the variable is non-zero because 
\begin_inset Formula $(SSR_{r}\geq SSR_{u})$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, One can increase the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 by simply 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

throwing in
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 more variables into the regression equation, even if they do not belong
 to the equation in theory.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To avoid this problem in assessing the goodness of the regression equation,
 the 
\series bold
adjusted 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 
\series default
(adjusted for the degrees of freedom) is often used.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\overline{R}^{2}=1-\frac{\nicefrac{(\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u})}{(N-K)}}{\nicefrac{(y-\bar{y})^{\prime}(y-\bar{y})}{(N-1)}}=1-\frac{N-1}{N-K}(1-R^{2});\ \ -\frac{K-1}{N-K}\leq\overline{R}^{2}\leq1\label{eq:26}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The Best Linear Unbiased Predictor
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider a linear regression model 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}=X_{1}\beta+u_{1}$
\end_inset

 and its OLS estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 which is the BLUE under assumptions (A1)-(A3).
 Suppose we wish to predict the value of the dependent variable at new values
 of regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

 when the regession relationship has not changed, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}=X_{2}\beta+u_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u=\left[\begin{array}{c}
u_{1}\\
u_{2}
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}:N\times1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}:N\times1$
\end_inset

, satisfies the assumptions (A1)-(A3),
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(uu^{\prime}|X)=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
E(u_{1}u_{1}^{\prime}|X) & E(u_{1}u_{2}^{\prime}|X)\\
E(u_{2}u_{1}^{\prime}|X) & E(u_{2}u_{2}^{\prime}|X)
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\sigma^{2}I_{N} & 0\\
0 & \sigma^{2}I_{N}
\end{array}\right]=\sigma^{2}I_{2N}
\]

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{1}u_{2}^{\prime}|X)=E(u_{2}u_{1}^{\prime}|X)=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To elaborate, we have the data on the new values of the regressors, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

, but we do not have data on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 However, we still wish to predict the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 To do this, we first obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 from the linear regression model 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}=X_{1}\beta_{1}+u_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we use this estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and the new values of the regressors, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

, to predict 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{2}=X_{2}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\emph on
The best linear unbiased predictor 
\series bold
(BLUP)
\series default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{2}=X_{2}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
(i) 
\series bold
Linearity
\series default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{2}$
\end_inset

 is a linear functionof 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is a linear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
(ii) 
\series bold
Unbiasedness
\series default
.
 The prediction error 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{2}=y_{2}-\hat{y}_{2}$
\end_inset

 has zero mean because
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(\hat{u}_{2}|X)=E[X_{2}(\beta-\hat{\beta})+u_{2}|X]=X_{2}(\beta-\beta)=0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
(iii) 
\series bold
Best
\series default
.
 The prediction error has the smallest covariance matrix.
 To show this,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(\hat{u}_{2}|X) & = & cov(y_{2}-\hat{y}_{2}|X)\\
 & = & cov(X_{2}(\beta-\hat{\beta})+u_{2}|X)\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}I_{N_{2}}+X_{2}cov(\hat{\beta}|X)X_{2}^{\prime}\ \ \mbox{(since \ensuremath{u_{2}}is uncorrelated with \ensuremath{X_{2}}and \ensuremath{(\beta-\hat{\beta})})}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}I_{N_{2}}+\sigma^{2}X_{2}(X_{1}^{\prime}X_{1})^{-1}X_{2}^{\prime}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Let another linear predictor be 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{2}=Ay_{1}$
\end_inset

 and prediction error 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{u}_{2}=y_{2}-\tilde{y}{}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 We need to choose 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 such that the predictor is unbiased, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\tilde{u}_{2})=0$
\end_inset

, and then we need to show 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\tilde{u}_{2})-cov(\hat{u}_{2})$
\end_inset

 is a positive semidefinite.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(\tilde{u}_{2}|X) & = & E(y_{2}-\tilde{y}_{2}|X)\\
 & = & E(X_{2}\beta+u_{2}-Ay_{1}|X)\\
 & = & E[X_{2}\beta+u_{2}-A(X_{1}\beta+u_{1})|X]\\
 & = & E(X_{2}\beta+u_{2}-AX_{1}\beta+Au_{1}|X)\\
 & = & 0\ \ \mbox{if}\ X_{2}=AX_{1}\\
cov(\tilde{u}_{2}|X) & = & cov(u_{2}+Au_{1}|X)\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}I_{N_{2}}+\sigma^{2}AA^{\prime}\\
cov(\tilde{u}_{2}|X)-cov(\hat{u}_{2}|X) & = & \sigma^{2}I_{N_{2}}+\sigma^{2}AA^{\prime}-[\sigma^{2}I_{N_{2}}+\sigma^{2}X_{2}(X_{1}^{\prime}X_{1})^{-1}X_{2}^{\prime}]\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}[AA^{\prime}-X_{2}(X_{1}^{\prime}X_{1})^{-1}X_{2}^{\prime}]\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}[AA^{\prime}-AX_{1}(X_{1}^{\prime}X_{1})^{-1}X_{1}^{\prime}A^{\prime}]\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}A[I-X_{1}(X_{1}^{\prime}X_{1})^{-1}X_{1}^{\prime}]A^{\prime}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}AM_{1}A^{\prime}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}(AM_{1})(AM_{1})^{\prime}\geq0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Statistical Inference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A major part of statistical inference is the test of hypothesis.
 A test of hypothesis consits of
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
specity the 
\emph on
null hypothesis
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $(H_{0})$
\end_inset

 and the 
\emph on
alternative hypothesis
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $(H_{1})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
choose a test statistic 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
choose a rejection region for a given level of significance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
conclude
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each hypothesis specifies the parameter space 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{1}$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{1}$
\end_inset

, then it is called a 
\series bold
\emph on
nested hypothesis
\series default
\emph default
; otherwise, it is called a 
\series bold
\emph on
nonnested hypothesis
\series default
\emph default
.
 A nested null (or maintained) hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:h(\theta)=0$
\end_inset

 against the alternative hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}:h(\theta)\neq0$
\end_inset

 restricts the parameter space.
 To test 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 against 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}$
\end_inset

, we need a 
\emph on
test statistic
\emph default
 that is computable from the sample (i.e., must not involve any unknown parameters
), and a decision rule of when to reject or not reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 The decision rule is expressed in terms of the 
\emph on
rejection region
\emph default
 (or 
\emph on
critical region
\emph default
), such that 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 is rejected if the test statistic is in the critial region.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the test statistic is a random variable, there is a positive probability
 that we will reject the true 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 for any reasonable choice of the critical region.
 Rejection of a true 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 is called a 
\emph on
Type I error
\emph default
, and the probability of Type I error is called the 
\emph on
size of a test
\emph default
, or the 
\emph on
significance level
\emph default
 of a test, commonly denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 The conventional choice of the significance level is 0.05 or 0.1.
 We may also fail to reject a false 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, which is called a 
\emph on
Type II error
\emph default
.
 The probability of rejecting a false 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\emph on
power of a test
\emph default
, which depends on the alternative hypothesis.
\end_layout

