\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
significance level:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=\mbox{Prob}\mbox{(reject a true \ensuremath{H_{0}})\ \ensuremath{=}\ Prob(type I error)}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Power of test:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=\mbox{Prob(reject a false \ensuremath{H_{0}})}=1-\mbox{Prob(not reject a false \ensuremath{H_{0}})}=1-\mbox{Prob(type II error)}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the significance level is the probability that the test statistic
 is in the rejection region when the null hypothesis is correct, we need
 to know the distribution function of the test statistic under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Similarly, the power of test requires the knowledge about the distribution
 function of the test statistic under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Since the distribution function of the test statistic depends on the true
 value of the parameters, there are many values of the power when the alternativ
e hypothesis is a composite hypothesis.
 Since the power of the test requires knowledge of the true population parameter
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, we are usually unable to compute it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An 
\emph on
ideal
\emph default
 
\emph on
test
\emph default
 (meaning an ideal choice of test statistic and critical region) is the
 one that has a zero probability of Type I and Type II errors.
 But such a test does not exist.
 Typically, choosing a smaller size of a test decreases the power of the
 test.
 A test is called the 
\emph on
most powerful test
\emph default
 if it has greater power than any other test of same test size, and the
 
\emph on
uniformly most powerful test
\emph default
 if it has greater power than any other test of same test size for all possible
 values of the parameters.
 A test is called an 
\emph on
unbiased test
\emph default
 if its power is at least as large as its test size.
 A test is called a 
\emph on
consistent test
\emph default
 if its power converges to 1 as the sample size grows to infinite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Finite Sample Tests of Nested Linear Hypotheses in a SLM
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Linear Regression Model: 
\begin_inset Formula $y=X\beta+u,\ u|X\sim N(0,\sigma^{2}I)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Number of observations: 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unrestricted OLS estimator:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \min_{\beta}}SSR_{u}=(y-X\beta)^{\prime}(y-X\beta)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Properties of Unrestricted OLSE:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\hat{\beta}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y\sim N(\beta,V),\ V=\sigma^{2}\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}\\
\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{SSR_{u}}{(N-K)},\ W_{u}\equiv\frac{(N-K)\hat{\sigma}^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}\sim\chi^{2}(N-K)\\
\hat{\beta}\ \mbox{is independent of}\ \hat{\sigma}^{2}\\
R\hat{\beta}\sim N(R\beta,S),\ S\equiv RVR^{\prime}=\sigma^{2}R(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}R^{\prime}\\
Z\equiv(R\hat{\beta}-q)^{\prime}S^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)\sim\chi^{2}(J;\delta)\\
F\equiv\frac{\nicefrac{Z}{J}}{\nicefrac{W_{u}}{(N-K)}}=\frac{1}{J}(R\hat{\beta}-q)^{\prime}\hat{S}^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)\sim F(J,N-K;\delta)\\
\delta=\left[(R\beta-q)^{\prime}S^{-1}(R\beta-q)\right]^{\nicefrac{1}{2}}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Resricted OLS estimator:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \min_{\beta}} & SSR_{r}=(y-X\beta)^{\prime}(y-X\beta)\\
\mbox{s.t.} & R\beta=q
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(J\times K)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(J\times1)$
\end_inset

 are known matrices, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}(R)=J<K$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Properties of Restricted OLSE
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\tilde{\beta}=\hat{\beta}-VR^{\prime}S^{-1}\left(R\hat{\beta}-q\right)\sim N\left(\beta-VR^{\prime}S^{-1}(R\beta-q),V-VR^{\prime}S^{-1}RV\right)\\
\tilde{\lambda}=\sigma^{2}S^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)\sim N\left(\sigma^{2}S^{-1}(R\beta-q),\sigma^{4}S^{-1}\right)\\
\tilde{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{SSR_{r}}{N-(K-J)}\\
W_{r}\equiv\frac{(N-K+J)\tilde{\sigma}^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}=\frac{SSR_{r}}{\sigma^{2}}\sim\chi^{2}(N-K+J;\delta)\\
\tilde{\beta},\ \tilde{\lambda},\ \tilde{\sigma}^{2}\ \mbox{are mutually independent}\\
SSR_{r}=SSR_{u}+\sigma^{2}(R\hat{\beta}-q)^{\prime}S^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Nested Linear Hypotheses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H_{0}:R\beta=q,\ H_{1}:R\beta\neq q\ \mbox{(or \ensuremath{H_{0}}is false)}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $J\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix of known constants and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $J\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of known constants.
 The restriction matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 has full row rank that is less than 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}(R)=J<K$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An intuition suggests that we may compare the unrestricted estimation 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and restricted estimation 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 to verify whether the restriction under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 is acceptable or not.
 These two estimators will be different with probability 1 due to the random
 variation of the sample.
 But, what do we expect about the magnitude of the difference? a big difference
 or small difference? It is easy to imagine that the difference will be
 relatively small (relative to their variances) if the restriciton under
 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 is correct and it is not binding in the minization of SSR.
 On the other hand, the difference is expected to be relatively large if
 the null hypothesis is incorrect and the null restriction is a binding
 constraint in the optimization.
 Then, it is reasonable to consider the difference 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}-\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 as a test statistic and construct a rejection region.
 Of course, we have to decide how to measure the difference because it is
 a 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-dimensional vector As we will discuss in more detail later, the Durbin-Wu-Hasum
an test can be interpreted as a test based on this type of reasoning, though
 it is used for different type of hypotheses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There is no reason why we should limit the test only to the comparison of
 the estimators themselves.
 It is possible to compare the estimated values of a certain function of
 parameters.
 Four tests presented below compare different functions of estimators.
 We will call them 
\emph on
likelihood ratio (LR) type test, Wald type test, Lagrange Multiplier (LM)
 type test
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
efficient score type test
\emph default
.
 The reason for the word 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
type
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is because the ideas of the finite sample tests discussed below are based
 on the ideas of the classical test procedures, but they are not the true
 LR, Wald, LM, and Score tests which are asymptotic tests and use the asymptotic
 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 distribution of the test statistics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Likelihood Ratio (LR) Type Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the least squares method tries to minimize the SSR, we may compare
 the values of the objective function (SSR).
 We know 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{r}$
\end_inset

 is greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{u}$
\end_inset

.
 Their difference is expected to be large if the restricitons under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 is not true and hence they are binding.
 Thus, it is intuitively reasonable to use the difference 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{r}-SSR_{u}$
\end_inset

 as the test statistic and we reject the null hypothesis if 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{r}-SSR_{u}$
\end_inset

 is large.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Once the test statistic is chosen, we need to determine the decision rule
 of when to reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 in favor of 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 As alluded to already, we will reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{r}-SSR_{u}$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
sufficiently large
\emph default
.
 Otherwise, the test concludes that the data does not provide enough evidence
 to reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 How big a difference is sufficiently large enough to reject the null hypothesis
? This requires a choice of the critical (rejection) region of the test.
 Since we reject the null hypothesis when 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{r}-SSR_{u}$
\end_inset

 is large, the proper critical region is the set of the sample 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{c}\left\{ SSR_{r}-SSR_{u}>c\right\} $
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is a pre-chosen value.
 The choice of the critical value depends on how much of Type I error we
 are willing to tolerate.
 A choice of large critical value will reject the null hypothesis less frequentl
y and we are more likely to commit the Type II error.
 The choice of the critical value is governed by the choice of 
\emph on
significance level (size of the test)
\emph default
.
 That is, for a pre-chosen significance level 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

, the crtical value is determined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(SSR_{r}-SSR_{u}>c|H_{0})=\alpha
\]

\end_inset

which requires knowledge of the distribution function of the test statistic
 under the null hypothesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
A small 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 leads to a higher critical value.
 So we reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 less frequently and we are more likely to commit the Type II error, so
 the power of the test decreases.
 The p-value is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p=\mbox{Prob(this sample\ |\ \ensuremath{H_{0}}\ is true)}
\]

\end_inset

It tells us how likely it is to get the sample we got if the null hypothesis
 is true.
 It is the probability of obtaining a test statistic at least as extremem
 as the one that was actually observed, assuming that the null hypothesis
 is true.
 It can also be interpreted as the chance of a Type I error if we reject
 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 If the p-value is less than 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

, then we reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, indicating that the observed result would be highly unlikely under the
 null hypothesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have shown
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Z\equiv\frac{SSR_{r}-SSR_{u}}{\sigma^{2}}=(R\hat{\beta}-q)^{\prime}S^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)\sim\chi^{2}(J;\delta)
\]

\end_inset

Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $S=\sigma^{2}R(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}R^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 involves the unknown parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, this is not a statistic (i.e, not computable from the sample).
 We may just replace 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 with its estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 But then, 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 does not have the 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 distribution.
 To see this, note that the replacement of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 with its estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is equivalent to taking a ratio of two independent 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 random variables, 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{u}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F\equiv\frac{\nicefrac{Z}{J}}{\nicefrac{W_{u}}{(N-K)}}=\frac{1}{J}(R\hat{\beta}-q)^{\prime}\hat{S}^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)\sim F(J,N-K;\delta)
\]

\end_inset

which can also be expressed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F\equiv\frac{\nicefrac{Z}{J}}{\nicefrac{W_{u}}{(N-K)}}=\frac{\nicefrac{(SSR_{r}-SSR_{u})}{J}}{\nicefrac{SSR_{u}}{(N-K)}}=\frac{N-K}{J}\frac{SSR_{r}-SSR_{u}}{SSR_{u}}\sim F(J,N-K;\delta)
\]

\end_inset

This is a statistic that has a central 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 distribution under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 and a noncentral 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 distribution under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 The null hypothesis is rejected if 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is greater than the critical value 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 of significance level 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $P(F>F_{\alpha}|H_{0})=\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 To compute this test statistic, we need both unrestricted estimators for
 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{u}$
\end_inset

 and restricted estimators for 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{r}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Wald type Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We know that restricted estimator satisfies the restriction under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $R\tilde{\beta}=q$
\end_inset

.
 The Wald type test asks whether the unrestricted estimator also satisfies
 the restriciton equation reasonably well.
 If the null hypothesis is correct so that the restricted and unrestricted
 estimators are close enough, we would exprec that 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is close to its hypothesized value 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

.
 If they are not close enough we will reject the null hypothesis.
 We amy also interpret this test as a comparison of 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}-q$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $R\tilde{\beta}-q$
\end_inset

, where the latter is a zero vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Unlike the scalar values of SSR's in the LR type test, 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $J\times1$
\end_inset

 vector in general.
 Therefore, we need to choose a measure of the distance of 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

.
 One may just consider the squared Euclidean norm, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert R\hat{\beta}-q\right\Vert ^{2}=\left(R\hat{\beta}-q\right)^{\prime}\left(R\hat{\beta}-q\right)$
\end_inset

.
 However, this does not take into account the differences in the variances
 of each element.
 We need to standardize 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}-q$
\end_inset

 in a certain way.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{\prime}P=S^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(R\hat{\beta}-q\right)\sim N(P(R\beta-q),I)$
\end_inset

.
 We now take the Euclidean norm of this standardized vector.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Z=\left\Vert P\left(R\hat{\beta}-q\right)\right\Vert ^{2}=\left(R\hat{\beta}-q\right)^{\prime}S^{-1}\left(R\hat{\beta}-q\right)\sim\chi^{2}(J;\delta)
\]

\end_inset

As in the previous case of the LR type test, we take the ratio of independent
 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 random variables to eliminate the known 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 The test statistic is then exactly the same as the LR type test statistic
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F\equiv\frac{1}{J}(R\hat{\beta}-q)^{\prime}\hat{S}^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)\sim F(J,N-K;\delta)
\]

\end_inset

Under the null hypothesis, this 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

-statistic distribution has a central 
\begin_inset Formula $F(J,N-K)$
\end_inset

.
 The null hypothesis is rejected if 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is greater than the critical value 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 of significance level 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $P(F>F_{\alpha}|H_{0})=\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 To compute this test statistic, we need only unrestricted estimators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Lagrange Multiplier (LM) type Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As the name indicates, this test is based on the estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}$
\end_inset

 of the Lagrange multiplier 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 If the restriction of 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 is true, it will not be a binding constraint and hence, the restricted
 estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}$
\end_inset

 (estimator of the shadow price of the restricitons) will be close to zero.
 Thus, we reject the null hypothesis if 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}$
\end_inset

 is sufficiently different from a zero vector.
 As in the Wald type test, we need to find a measure of the distance between
 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}$
\end_inset

 and a zero vector because 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}$
\end_inset

 is a vector in general.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Following the same procedure as in the Wald type test, we note
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Z_{r}\equiv\tilde{\lambda}^{\prime}\left[\sigma^{4}S^{-1}\right]^{-1}\tilde{\lambda}=\sigma^{-4}\tilde{\lambda}^{\prime}S\tilde{\lambda}=\sigma^{-2}\tilde{\lambda}^{\prime}\left[R(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}R^{\prime}\right]\tilde{\lambda}\sim\chi^{2}(J;\delta)
\]

\end_inset

and we take the ratio of independent 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 random variables to eliminate the unknown 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F_{r}=\frac{\nicefrac{Z_{r}}{J}}{\nicefrac{W_{r}}{(N-K+J)}}=\frac{1}{J}\tilde{\sigma}^{-2}\tilde{\lambda}^{\prime}\left[R(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}R^{\prime}\right]\tilde{\lambda}\sim F(J,N-K+J)\ \mbox{under}\ H_{0}
\]

\end_inset

Note that this test statistic requires only restricted estimators, and its
 degrees of freedom are different from those of the LR and Wald type tests.
 Under the null hypothesis, this has a central 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 distribution because both the numerator and denominator terms are distributed
 as a central 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 However, under the alternative hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $(R\beta\neq q)$
\end_inset

, both 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{r}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{r}$
\end_inset

 are noncentral 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 random variables with the same noncentrality parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{r}$
\end_inset

 has a 
\emph on
doubly-noncentral 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 distribution
\emph default
 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 This will affect the power of the test.
 The critical value for the rejection region is still found from the null
 central 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have shown earlier that the LR type test and the Wald type test are identical.
 To see the relationship of the LM type test with other tests, note the
 solution 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}=\sigma^{2}S^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $S^{-1}$
\end_inset

 has a full column rank, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}\approx0$
\end_inset

 is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}-q\approx0$
\end_inset

, which is the Wald type test.
 Therefore, both tests are checking on the same quantity.
 This becomes clear if 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}=\sigma^{2}S^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)$
\end_inset

 is substituted into 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{r}$
\end_inset

 equation.
 The resulting equation is 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{r}=(R\hat{\beta}-q)^{\prime}S^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-q)$
\end_inset

, which is the same as 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, ther numerator terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{r}$
\end_inset

 are the same.
 The difference is the denominator term.
 If we use the unrestricted estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 instead of the restricted estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

, that is, if we use 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{W_{u}}{(N-K)}$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{W_{r}}{(N-K+J)}$
\end_inset

 in the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{r}$
\end_inset

, then we have a test statistic that is the same as the Wald type test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Efficient Score type Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The unrestricted estimator is the solution of the first order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left.\frac{\partial SSR}{\partial\beta}\right|_{\beta=\hat{\beta}}=-2X^{\prime}y+2X^{\prime}X\hat{\beta}=0\implies g(\hat{\beta})=-X^{\prime}y+X^{\prime}X\hat{\beta}=0
\]

\end_inset

If the restrictions are correct, then the restricted estimator must satisfy
 this equation closely so that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
g(\tilde{\beta})=-X^{\prime}y+X^{\prime}X\tilde{\beta}=0
\]

\end_inset

The score type test thus asks whether the restricted estimator satisfies
 the first order condition of the unrestricted least squares estimation.
 The test rejects the null hypothesis if 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\tilde{\beta})$
\end_inset

 is far from a zero vector.
 This test can also be considered as a comparison of restricted gradient
 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\tilde{\beta})$
\end_inset

 and unrestricted 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

 which is of course zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The score type test is equivalent to the LM type test.
 Consider the first order condition of the restricted least squares
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-X^{\prime}y+X^{\prime}X\tilde{\beta}+R^{\prime}\tilde{\lambda}=0\equiv g(\tilde{\beta})+R^{\prime}\tilde{\lambda}=0
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 has a full column rank by our specification of the null hypothesis, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}=0$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\tilde{\beta})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the LM type test of asking whether 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}$
\end_inset

 is close to zero or not is equivalent to asking whether 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\tilde{\beta})$
\end_inset

 is close to zero or not.
 Substitution of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{\prime}\tilde{\lambda}=-g(\tilde{\beta})$
\end_inset

 into the 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{r}$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Z_{r}=\sigma^{-2}\tilde{\lambda}^{\prime}\left[R(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}R^{\prime}\right]\tilde{\lambda}=\sigma^{-2}\left(R^{\prime}\tilde{\lambda}\right)^{\prime}\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}\left(R^{\prime}\tilde{\lambda}\right)=\sigma^{-2}g(\tilde{\beta})^{\prime}\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}g(\tilde{\beta})
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F_{r}=\frac{1}{J}\tilde{\sigma}^{-2}g(\tilde{\beta})^{\prime}\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}g(\tilde{\beta})\sim F(J,N-K+J)\ \mbox{under}\ H_{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Case of Single Restriction: 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $J=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is a scalar, then under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t^{*}=\frac{\nicefrac{\left(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta\right)}{\mbox{sd}(R\hat{\beta})}}{\sqrt{\nicefrac{W}{(N-K)}}}=\frac{\nicefrac{\left(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta\right)}{\mbox{sd}(R\hat{\beta})}}{\sqrt{\nicefrac{\hat{\sigma}^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}}}=\frac{\left(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta\right)}{\mbox{est. sd}(R\hat{\beta})}\sim t(N-K)
\]

\end_inset

and under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t=\frac{(R\hat{\beta}-q)}{\mbox{est. sd}(R\hat{\beta})}\sim t(N-K,R\beta-q)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 is rejected when 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|t\right|>t_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 where the critical value is determined by 
\begin_inset Formula $P(\left|t\right|>t_{\alpha}|H_{0})=\alpha$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_body
